<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Memory disaggregation via Compute Express Link (CXL) enables multiple hosts to share remote memory, improving utilization for data-intensive workloads.
Today, virtual memory enables process-level isolation on a host and CXL enables host-level isolation.
This creates a critical security gap: the absence of process-level memory isolation in shared disaggregated memory.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We present Space-Control, a hardware-software co-design that provides fine-grained, process-level isolation for shared disaggregated memory.
Space-Control authenticates execution context in the hardware and enforces access control on every memory access and amortizes lookup times with a small cache.
Our design allows up to 127 processes running concurrently on 255 hosts to share memory with only 1.56% storage overhead.
In a gem5 + Structural Simulation Toolkit (SST) based CXL model, Space-Control incurs minimal performance overhead of 3.3%, making shared disaggregated memory isolation practical.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The growing demand for memory in modern data-intensive applications has driven the industry toward memory disaggregation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Felten and Zahorjan, 1991</a>)</cite>.
Disaggregation decouples compute and memory resources and connects them via high-speed fabrics.
Standards such as the Compute Express Link (CXL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> enable multiple systems, called <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hosts</span>, to access memory on a remote memory-only <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">device</span>.
This improves utilization and reduces redundancy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>.
The CXL 3.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>)</cite> standard supports memory sharing at the cache-line granularity across hosts for the first time, making it a promising foundation for large-scale deployments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Current CXL-based sharing mechanisms provide only coarse-grained, host-level access control: <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">once a host is authorized, all processes on that host can access the shared region.</span>
This violates the <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">principle of least privilege</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lampson, 1974</a>)</cite>.
The <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all-or-nothing</span> model leaves intra-host isolation to the operating system (OS).
Since memory is shared across multiple hosts, there is no cross-host user or process visibility.
Stark <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stark et al., 2023</a>)</cite> call the coarse-grained sharing in CXL as <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“sledgehammer-level protections.”</span>
When coupled with a compromised kernel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google Inc., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kocher et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bains and Halbert, 2012</a>)</cite>, this gap can escalate privileges and access sensitive data, creating a significant security risk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Before discussing the problem in details, we first need to consider the characteristics of shared memory workloads.
Disaggregated memory workloads are an active research topic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">MICRON, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
These worloads partition their data across local and remote memory.
Although multiple hosts may map the same physical remote memory region via CXL, they are not necessarily accessing the same data within that region.
Therefore, for isolation, we need to guarantee <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fine-grained</span> permissions for user-defined <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">arbitrary</span> memory ranges for the remote memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Tigon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, a CXL-based key-value store software, optimizes fine-grained data movement and resource utilization in the remote memory across hosts.
The authors point out a lack of access control in CXL and implicitly assumed the existence of fine-grained permissions to enforce isolation across shared regions.
Beyond key-value stores, graph analytics is an another example where users share graphs or sub-graphs in the remote memory across hosts, each running a graph kernel.
This requires fine-grained authorization such that users on a host can read or update only its assigned partitions (<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, specific compressed sparse row slices or property arrays) without exposing the rest of the graph.
Other examples include the sharing of machine learning model weights (especially in expert models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>) across hosts to improve utilization and reduce redundancy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Today, systems enforce such policies either at the OS level or at the software level, without hardware-backed guarantees.
This motivates us towards researching hardware-enforced isolation that remains effective even if the OS is compromised.
In principle, we need guarantees akin to confidential computing, namely, creating an enclave for isolation of the address space and independence from the OS.
In practice however, current Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) are ill-suited to shared disaggregated memory as commercial TEE designs like Intel SGX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2015</a>)</cite>, Intel TDX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2022</a>)</cite>, AMD SEV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, CXL-TEE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>)</cite> <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">etc.</span> cannot share memory between enclaves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Today’s TEEs have security monitors that operate only within a single host, with no cross-host coordination, making it challenging for existing TEE designs to securely share remote memory across hosts.
Furthermore, they introduce notable performance overheads, reduce deployment flexibility, and expand the trusted computing base (TCB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akram et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
This leads us to the central research question in this paper:</p>
<blockquote id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_p">“How can we ensure isolation in shared disaggregated memory
that enforces the principle of least privilege and operates
independently of the OS with minimal overhead?”</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present a hardware-backed access control framework that enforces isolation defined by a <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">process</span>.
A process is the smallest OS abstraction that issues user-mode loads/stores (LD/ST) under its own distinct address space.
Further, our design ensures compatibility with the virtual memory infrastructure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Across the ecosystem, existing access control mechanisms fail to provide the necessary granularity and trust guarantees for disaggregated environments.
The POSIX shared memory API <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>)</cite> solved inter-process shared memory on a single host.
However, cross-host usergroups may differ, so POSIX shared memory does not address cross-host sharing.
Software-only approaches such as <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">namespaces</span> and <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cgroups</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>)</cite> offer isolation, but requires trusting the OS for cross-host visibility. Hardware-only solutions like DeACT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and the CXL specification support coarse-grained sharing (1 GiB and 256 MiB respectively), limiting flexibility.
Prior proposals based on capabilities, which guarantee isolation, argue for finer access-control at the object-level for CXL fabrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stark et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
However, capability systems like CHERI demand pervasive CPU/ISA/compiler/OS changes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watson et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Woodruff et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bresniker et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The core difficulty is balancing fine-grained authorization with scalability and efficiency.
Prior works have emphasized <em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">horizontal</em> access-control, <span id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> host-level permissions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>)</cite>, whereas disaggregated memory also needs <em id="S1.p8.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">vertical</em> access-control, <span id="S1.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> per-process or per-virtual machine across hosts.
Tagging every 4 KiB page of a 1 TiB memory region with permissions (2 bits) per host (up to 256) and per process or per-virtual machine (up to 128) permissions requires 2 TiB of metadata (200% overhead).
Therefore, naïve lookup table designs become infeasible, and permission checks risk becoming performance bottlenecks.
Prior research like Mondrian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Witchel et al., 2002</a>)</cite> trades off storage overhead for higher lookup latency on a single host using sorted tables to solve this problem.
However, in shared disaggregated memory (SDM) each host’s permission table must be replicated for cross-host visibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we require a solution that provides shared memory isolation without incurring prohibitive metadata or performance overhead.
We present Space-Control, a scalable access-control framework for isolation in shared disaggregated memory.
Space-Control registers and authenticates address spaces defined by a user process at the host using a secure process attribute context engine (SPACE), instead of the OS.
Further, we add a permission checker to enforces access-control on every LD/ST at the point of egress.
Finally, we leverage the under-utilized fabric manager (FM) in CXL for managing cryptographic keys and permissions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Access control metadata is co-designed with the kernel to achieve scalability. This reduces the total storage overhead of prior approaches from 200% to a fixed 1.56% of the total memory capacity when 255 hosts share memory of 4 KiB pages across 127 process on each host.
Our design delivers hardware-backed isolation with space-efficient metadata, addressing both the flexibility and granularity requirements of the remote memory.
Evaluation on GAPBS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beamer et al., 2017</a>)</cite> shows a marginal 3.3% performance overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The contributions of this paper are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify a critical isolation gap in shared disaggregated memory (SDM).
While current systems provide process-level isolation (via the OS) and host-level isolation (via CXL), they lack process-level isolation for SDM.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose Space-Control, a system providing hardware-enforced, process-level isolation for SDM that remains confidential even if the OS is compromised.
This architecture includes a hardware validation module and an access-control permission checker.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We address metadata overhead through a hardware-software co-designed permissions management technique.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the hardware design of Space-Control and provide a quantitative evaluation of its performance and space overheads.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of the paper is organized as follows.
§<a href="#S2" title="2. Problem Definition ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> defines the problem and §<a href="#S3" title="3. Background and Current Approaches ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> explains the background.
§<a href="#S4" title="4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> describes our solution and §<a href="#S5" title="5. Discussion ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> discusses the implications of the solution.
The following sections discusses the evaluation methodology and results. §<a href="#S8" title="8. Related Works ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> explains related works.
The work is concluded in §<a href="#S9" title="9. Conclusion ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Problem Definition</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Threat Model</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Principle and Scope.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Space-Control’s trust model follows the principle of least privilege <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lampson, 1974</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saltzer and Schroeder, 1975</a>)</cite>. Only address spaces defined by a process, that require access to the remote memory, should be allowed to access the same.
We provide <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span> to local and remote pages of such process’ data.
Further, we provide <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">integrity</span> to all remote pages and detect any intrusion by the OS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">System setting.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a HPC-like deployment where several multi-tenant hosts share SDM via CXL.
Users directly interact with the host OS via user processes.
Applications interact with SDM through user processes that map regions (<span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, DAX).
Each process has both local (on-host) pages and remote (SDM) pages.
We do not support virtual machines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Trusted.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We trust the user process (its code and keys) that is granted SDM access, and the additional hardware we propose.
We assume on-chip and CXL hardware to function correctly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Untrusted.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The OS (including the kernel) is untrusted in the kernel mode (or <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">S-mode</span>).
Our only kernel change is a narrow extension that enables the OS to understand process contexts and SDM ranges; it contains no authorization logic.
If this change is absent, SDM remains unmapped and the hardware permission checker rejects all SDM requests.
Other user or kernel processes on the host may collude with the OS to break confidentiality.
Off-chip components such as local DRAM modules and other external devices are untrusted.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attacker Capabilities.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a powerful adversary capable of controlling the OS.
OS-level vulnerabilities can escalate privileges and override permissions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kocher et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipp et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google Inc., </a>)</cite>.
The attacker can manipulate page tables, virtual memory mappings, replay or forge identity, and invoke privileged instructions.
Physical attacks, denial-of-service and side-channels on the hardware are out of scope.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Requirements</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the scope and the threat model, any given solution needs to have the following requirements:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">R1</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It should enforce access control per process and not per host, ensuring the <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">principle of least privilege</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">R2</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The solution needs to operate independently of the OS, so that isolation remains intact even if the OS is compromised, <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">thereby minimizing the TCB</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">R3</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design must scale to large number of hosts and processes with <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">low metadata footprint</span> and small per-access overhead.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">R4</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Allow dynamic creation/teardown of <span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shared</span> memory ranges and policy changes across processes.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Background and Current Approaches</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section explains access-control on a single-host system and describes the missing gap in disaggregated memory systems.
Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3. Background and Current Approaches ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the related works in terms of the four requirements mentioned in §<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Requirements ‣ 2. Problem Definition ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On a single-host system, the virtual memory manages per-process page tables.
The hardware (TLB/MMU) enforces translation and per-page <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">W/R/X</span> permissions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arpaci-Dusseau and Arpaci-Dusseau, 2018</a>)</cite>.
A process cannot access another’s pages unless they are explicitly shared.
Address space identifier (ASID; ARM) or process context identifier (PCID; X86) tags TLB entries to distinguish address spaces and reduce shootdowns.
They aid performance but do not provide authorization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Intel’s PASID/SVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Intel, </a>)</cite> binds I/O accelerators to a process’ virtual address space for device-initiated DMA via the IOMMU.
This is orthogonal to CPU-originated LD/ST via <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DAX</span>.
OS-managed mechanisms such as Border-Control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olson et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, MPK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and ARM MTE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">ARM, Technical report</a>)</cite> provide intra-host memory protection but assume a trusted kernel. Mondrian memory protection defines <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">domains</span> as a set of processes with identical permissions, defined by the OS.
These domains are sorted and stored as separate entries on a permission table.
TEEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2016</a>)</cite> provide isolation and attestation, but are ill-suited for sharing across hosts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>)</cite> and add significant overhead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akram et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:165.6pt;height:41.1pt;vertical-align:-19.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-241.9pt,60.0pt) scale(0.25502760988797,0.25502760988797) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
