<th id="S3.T1.2.1.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_rr ltx_border_t">Space-Control</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.12.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.12.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.12.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_rr ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.12.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">✓</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>The table highlights that Space-Control is the only approach that achieves process-level isolation, OS independence, scalability, and efficiency simultaneously, while minimizing the trusted computing base.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Disaggregated memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Felten and Zahorjan, 1991</a>)</cite> decouples compute from memory and lets hosts map remote ranges over a fabric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguilera et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
The fabric manager (FM) manages the set of hosts sharing memory on a set of devices.
Prior efforts include OpenCAPI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">IBM, 2014</a>)</cite> and Gen-Z <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Interconnect, 2017</a>)</cite>, but current deployments center on CXL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>.
The critical difference for our work is the <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">translation and authorization path</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Gen-Z introduced a host-side zMMU and two-stage translation (virtual address (VA) <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!\to\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!\to\!</annotation></semantics></math> remote virtual address <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!\to\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!\to\!</annotation></semantics></math> physical address (PA)), which enabled designs like DeACT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>)</cite> to decouple translation from access-control.
In contrast, CXL removes the host-side zMMU and reuses the CPU’s TLB to access remote memory directly.
With CXL 3.0, multiple hosts may share the same physical memory range <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>)</cite> (called <span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sharing</span>).
However, authorization is <em id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">host-level</em> and coarse (dynamic capacity region granularity) under the FM. In practice, to avoid the OS zeroing-out the bits, an SDM range is mapped as a direct access (DAX) region, similar to the heterogeneous programming model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">MICRON, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bresniker et al., 2019</a>)</cite> either target the Gen-Z model or rely on a trusted OS for isolation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shan et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.
CHERI-style capability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watson et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Woodruff et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bresniker et al., 2019</a>)</cite> systems offer principled, fine-grained authority tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stark et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, but adoption requires ISA/compiler/OS changes across all hosts.
CHERI uses 128 bit pointers to maintain capability per host, maintaining the metadata across 256 hosts take 12.5% more storage (<math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 1b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 1b</annotation></semantics></math> per 16 Bytes).
In addition, programs needs to be rewritten to understand <span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">capabilities-aware</span> LD/ST instructions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Woodruff et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.
We instead design a process-level authorization path compatible with existing virtual memory infrastructure and CXL deployments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Space-Control</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Space-Control enforces <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per-process</em> authorization (R1) for shared disaggregated memory outside the OS’ control (R2).
We achieve this by
<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> authenticating the currently executing process,
<span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> tagging its memory requests with a hardware-backed process identifier, and,
<span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(iii)</span> validating each SDM access against a permission table.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Space-Control introduces three key components:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Secure Process Attribute Context Engine (SPACE):</span>
A lightweight hardware module that authenticates processes using hardware-rooted identity, defined by the <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hardware process context</span>, <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> reserved PCID/ASID, and, the pointer to the base of the page table (<span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CR3/SATP/TTBR</span>); referred to as <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>.
The engine monitors context switches and ensures that only verified contexts can access the SDM.
It offers minimal MMIO interface to the programmer.
In addition, it has logic for generating cryptographic labels, and, a counter.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Permission Table:</span>
An array of permission entries that maps PA ranges; shared or exclusive; of the SDM to the set of authorized contexts.
Entries can be dynamically updated to satisfy R4.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Permission Checker:</span>
An on-chip hardware unit placed after the last-level cache, before the local DRAM controller and the CXL downstream port, responsible for enforcing access control on each LD/ST. It is integrated into the memory access pipeline that actively validates each SDM request against the <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">permission table</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Space-Control makes a key observation in existing works to, <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> access control metadata increases to maintain each host’s processes that are allowed to access the remote memory (estimated to be 200% in the naïve approach; §<a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, 12.5% in CHERI; §<a href="#S3" title="3. Background and Current Approaches ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Therefore, we opt for a hardware-software co-design, explained in §<a href="#S4.SS2.SSS2" title="4.2.2. Permission Table ‣ 4.2. Space-Control Components ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2.2</span></a>, to satisfy R3.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We minimally modify the kernel to reserve contiguous process context IDs (ASID/PCIDs (referred to a <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span> henceforth) instead of Linux PID) in the kernel for SDM processes.
These <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPIDs</span> are assigned by the SPACE, instead of the OS.
The OS still remains untrusted as our correctness never depends on kernel behavior.
If the OS is unmodified or these extensions are absent, SDM cannot be mapped or accessed, and the permission checker will always reject any SDM traffic</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The FM serves as a trusted entity for cryptographic keys and permissions management.
Its secret key (<math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{FM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{FM}</annotation></semantics></math>) is used to generate public authentication token labels (<math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math>) for host process context validation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/design-overview.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/239;" width="381" height="239"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Space-Control architecture providing process-level isolation for shared disaggregated memory.
With the addition of new trusted hardware, only the trusted process P1 on host3 is allowed access to the shared memory.
P2 (malicious, red) and P3 (untrusted, white) processes are not authorized to access the shared memory.
The same trusted hardware is also present in host0 and host1, where other trusted processes are allowed to access the shared memory.
In CXL however, any process authorized by the kernel can access that data.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the system-level architecture of Space-Control.
While green in general represents trusted components, the darker shade represents additional hardware.
The FM manages the permission table, stored in SDM.
The key difference between Space-Control and CXL is that, in our design, neither process P2 nor the kernel on host3 can access the remote memory, whereas in CXL any process authorized by the kernel can access that data.
Each host enforces access control through SPACE, which authenticates contexts using hardware-rooted identity.
The permission checker validates every memory request against the permission table before committing.
Only trusted contexts with legitimate permissions can issue LD/ST, while unauthorized processes are blocked, ensuring OS-independent security.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Principle of Operation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now that we have given an overview of the design, we explain the work flow in three distinct phases:
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> process creation,
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> runtime protection, and,
<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(c)</span> dynamic updates.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Process Creation</h4>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/process-creation.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/168;" width="471" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Process creation and permission grant workflow in Space-Control.
A new process requests access to SDM, which proposes an entry to the metadata region.
The FM is automatically notified and decides approval, commits the proposed entry to the table, and issues <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1.1. Process Creation ‣ 4.1. Principle of Operation ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the process creation and permission request workflow in Space-Control.
Space-Control scopes to protect user processes to satisfy R1.
The user registers their process as trusted via SPACE hardware instead of the OS, and, creates an entry in the permission table to ensure permission enforcement.
Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> requests a range of memory to be mapped into a trusted process’ address space, running on the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> host.
The user is responsible for requesting the right permissions.
This can be achieved either using a <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">middleware</span> or an <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">upper layer protocol</span> as mentioned by prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SPACE maintains a list of free HWPIDs assigned via an MMIO doorbell (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GET_NEXT_PID()</span>).
The system-level driver assigns a HWPID from the free list. This breaks the dependence on the OS for a process identifier (R2).
The permission table that maintains each permission entry is stored in the SDM.
A new entry (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">entry_t</span>) is written into the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proposed update</span> section of the permission table directly (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The FM is automatically notified about the update, and decides whether to approve the request.
If the entry is approved, the FM commits the entry (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>) into the table.
The FM immediately generates a cryptographic public label (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math>) using its private key (K<sub id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_sub">FM</sub>) for authentication and stores it into the permission table (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">5</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>).
The FM automatically optimizes the permission entry if entries’ ranges overlap.
In addition, it responds to the host with the public label (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">6</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>), which is intercepted by SPACE for cryptographic label comparison.
The user process is now assumed to be trusted and the execution continues.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Runtime Protection</h4>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/runtime-v2.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/171;" width="471" height="171"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Flow of remote memory access as a load/store (LD/ST) of a virtual address (VA) in Space-Control.
SPACE validates the context, and the permission checker enforces isolation.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now have a registered process, which we consider trusted.
However, multi-tenant OS has other processes running in the system.
Therefore, we need to validate our trusted process at every context switch to maintain R1.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1.2. Runtime Protection ‣ 4.1. Principle of Operation ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> depicts the runtime protections provided by Space-Control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When a new process is context switched (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>), the SPACE logic, implemented in a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sequencer</span>, automatically reads the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span> (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CR3/SATP/TTBR</span>) and the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span> (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pcid/ASID</span>), which defines the current process’ <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context</span>.
Based on the current context, the trusted user process calls MMIO doorbell <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARM_LABEL</span> to generate a host-side label (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math>) from the user-space.
SPACE generates <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iff</span> <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARM_LABEL</span> is invoked from user-space eliminating the possibility of the kernel falsely trying to validate a different context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> is loaded into a shadow register called the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">label register</span> (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>).
A context is validated if the current label <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> matches with the public label from the FM <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The CPU core has a hierarchical, hardware-enforced CPU privilege mechanism called protection rings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schroeder and Saltzer, 1972</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karger and Herbert, 1984</a>)</cite>.
These are used to restrict access protection rings for machine, hypervisor, supervisor (OS/kernel) and user-level program <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akram, 2023</a>)</cite>.
To avoid OS traps, kernel isolation, and stale labels, the shadow register is automatically unset if the core’s protection ring is anything other than the user-space.
For any trusted context, all PA (local or remote) generated by the same is extended to tag the HWPID bits.
We call these HWPID bits the authentication bits (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">4</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> shows LD/ST instructions. The permission checker checks the A-bits for all LD/ST.
SDM LD/ST always need to have the A-bits set (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">5</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>).
To validate whether the given context has permissions to access the PA, a permission request (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">6</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>) along with the LD/ST (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic4" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.pic4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">7</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>) is sent to the SDM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The permission response has a range and the HWPIDs of authenticated processes.
When both responses arrive (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">8</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> and Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.pic2" class="ltx_picture" height="10.83" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-1.4px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 10.83 10.83" width="10.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,10.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(5.41,0) translate(0,5.41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 5.14 0 C 5.14 2.84 2.84 5.14 0 5.14 C -2.84 5.14 -5.14 2.84 -5.14 0 C -5.14 -2.84 -2.84 -5.14 0 -5.14 C 2.84 -5.14 5.14 -2.84 5.14 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -3.2 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="6.4"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.pic2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">9</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>), permission checks are enforced based on the LD/ST address and the tagged <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span> (Action <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.pic3" class="ltx_picture" height="15.65" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-3.81px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 15.65 15.65" width="15.65"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,15.65) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.83,0) translate(0,7.83)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000"><path d="M 7.55 0 C 7.55 4.17 4.17 7.55 0 7.55 C -4.17 7.55 -7.55 4.17 -7.55 0 C -7.55 -4.17 -4.17 -7.55 0 -7.55 C 4.17 -7.55 7.55 -4.17 7.55 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -6.4 -4.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.03)" width="12.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.pic3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">10</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>).
An interrupt is generated if there is an access violation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure local memory pages of the trusted process are confidential at all times, a memory encryption engine is used.
The <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span> of a validated context are always tagged, which cannot be forged by the OS. Even if a malicious OS tampers with the trusted process’s page-table entries, any data it can exfiltrate from local memory is ciphertext, thus maintaining <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Revocation and Update Propagation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We rely on CXL’s back invalidates (issued via <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BISnp</span>) to propagate updates to the permission table.
When an update is committed to the table by the FM, every host automatically receives a <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BISnp</span>, ensuring that the permission is invalid even if the entry is cached in the permission cache.
The FM updates the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math> for the same host, ensuring global transparency and satisfying R4 of dynamically updating permissions.
The driver is responsible for calling SPACE doorbells for cleaning up HWPIDs.
Permission entries with no hosts are automatically cleaned up by the FM.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Space-Control Components</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now explain each component within the workflow mentioned in §<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Principle of Operation ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, describing their individual microarchitectures.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1. </span>Secure Process Attribute Context Engine (SPACE)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SPACE, shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.1. Secure Process Attribute Context Engine (SPACE) ‣ 4.2. Space-Control Components ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, is the hardware root of trust for process authentication.
It binds a process context using hardware-rooted identity (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>).
It stores the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math> and a list of free <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPIDs</span>.
SPACE ensures that only processes explicitly authorized by the FM can access SDM.
It has a <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Sequencer</span>, that monitors context switches and generates cryptographic labels.
Further, provides MMIO doorbells for the OS system to interact.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/space-micro.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:471/383;" width="471" height="383"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The microarchitecture of the SPACE.
It stores the host’s secret key (<math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{host}</annotation></semantics></math>), FM’s public label (<math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math>) and a free <span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span> list.
There is additional logic to generate <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, SPACE provides minimal MMIO doorbells available to the OS for HWPID management.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To establish context validation, Space-Control uses two complementary labels:
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> the FM-issued public label (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math>), and
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> the SPACE generated local context label (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math>).
These labels are generated using standard MAC primitive (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span> HMAC-SHA-256 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krawczyk et al., 1997</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nyström, 2005</a>)</cite> or AES-CMAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Daemen and Rijmen, 1998</a>)</cite>).
There are hardware implementation of such algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Juliato and Gebotys, 2011</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During the process creation phase (§<a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1. Process Creation ‣ 4.1. Principle of Operation ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a>), the FM generates <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math> that acts as an authorization token that binds a process (on a specific host) to the memory range(s) it may access.
Conceptually,</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}=MAC_{K_{FM}}(host\_id,HWPID,BASE\_P,range)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}=MAC_{K_{FM}}(host\_id,HWPID,BASE\_P,range)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{FM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{FM}</annotation></semantics></math> is the FM’s secret key.
SPACE stores the authorization label for subsequent checks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, the SPACE-generated host-side label <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated and stored in the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">label register</span> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iff</span> the authorized user-space process invokes <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ARM_LABEL</span> at a context switch (§<a href="#S4.SS1.SSS2" title="4.1.2. Runtime Protection ‣ 4.1. Principle of Operation ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.2</span></a>).
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the identity of the process currently running on that core.
Freshness of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> is ensured via a monotonic counter. <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}=MAC_{K_{host}}(BASE\_P,HWPID,ctr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}=MAC_{K_{host}}(BASE\_P,HWPID,ctr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> is the host’s secret key known to the SPACE, and ctr is the monotonic counter that advances on each context activation per core.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, on a context switch, SPACE:</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Derives the current process identity, or the <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context</span>, from the active address-space root (<span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span>, <span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generates <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> and stores it in the <span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">label register</span> for that specific core.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compares the two labels to match the predicate.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">If authentication succeeds, each LD/ST issued by the active context is marked as authenticated by tagging the A-bits.
This allows per-access permission enforcement downstream, thus achieveing R1, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> adhering to the principle of least privileges. This inherently also prevents synonyms in the memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">SPACE satisfies R2, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> ensures OS independence.
All identity construction and label checks occur in trusted hardware and the OS cannot mint identities or forge labels.
The monotonic counter ensures that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{host}</annotation></semantics></math> is tied to exactly one context activation, which prevents replay attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei and Pu, 2005</a>)</cite>.
Further, this ensures the principle of least privilege as authorization tokens bind contexts to specific SDM ranges.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.06951v1/permission-table.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/198;" width="471" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Layout of the permission table stored in the SDM.
The permission table starts at an offset 128 B (cache-line aligned).
It stores sorted permission entries (entry_t) keyed by start address.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span>Permission Table</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The permission table, shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2.1. Secure Process Attribute Context Engine (SPACE) ‣ 4.2. Space-Control Components ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, stores access control metadata per process per host as entries.
Each entry has a start, a size, and, permission attributes per process per host.
It functions as an optimized reverse mapping table, inspired by directory-based caching protocols, that maps a PA to a host and a process within the host.
The metadata section of the table allows hosts to propose new updates to the table.
The rest of the table with all the permission entries is only accessible to the FM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The organization of the permission table is central to ensuring scalability and satisfying requirement R3.
The primary challenge is maintaining unique pairs of (host, process identifier) across all participating hosts.
To solve this, we maintain two copies of the HWPIDs authorized to access the SDM. The local copy, denoted by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HWPID_{local}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HWPID_{local}</annotation></semantics></math>, contains the HWPIDs assigned to trusted processes on a given host.
The permission table copy, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HWPID_{global}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HWPID_{global}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents the union of all trusted processes across all hosts and is defined as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HWPID_{global}=\bigcup_{i}HWPID_{local_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HWPID_{global}=\bigcup_{i}HWPID_{local_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The intersection <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HWPID_{local}\bigcap HWPID_{global}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HWPID_{local}\bigcap HWPID_{global}</annotation></semantics></math> validates the right context at the right host.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A process on a host can request memory range via a <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">request</span> call.
The call writes a new entry to the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proposed update</span> section.
The FM commits the entry into the table <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iff</span> permission is granted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The permission table is organized as a sorted table based on the start addresses.
This is similar to prior works like Mondrian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Witchel et al., 2002</a>)</cite>, where each domain has a sorted segment table.
This allows flexibility in defining protections for arbitrary memory ranges instead of fixed page lengths (R1).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3. </span>Permission Checker</h4>

<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/permission-micro.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:471/433;" width="471" height="433"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6. </span>The microarchitecture of the permission checker.
The diagram shows the flow of a 41 bit PA (48 bit extended PA).
While every SDM LD/ST is validated against the permission table, local pages are encrypted to ensure confidentiality.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The permission checker, shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2.3. Permission Checker ‣ 4.2. Space-Control Components ‣ 4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is designed in tandem with the previous components.
It is placed on-chip of a host, after the last-level cache. Every LD/ST of a trusted process has the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span> tagged on to the PA.
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HWPID_{local}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HWPID_{local}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> the list of trusted processes on a host, is implemented as a bit vector, and stored in the permission checker.
The checker validates whether the trusted process’ tagged addresses have access rights to a given PA, thus maintaining R1 and R2 at all times.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At runtime, every SDM LD/ST is associated with a corresponding permission entry in the table that encodes the range and permission attributes.
The checker fetches the same entry from the permission table.
Homonyms never appear in our design as PA are used for range checks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The lookup latency is proportional to the number of permission entries.
To amortize the lookup latency and avoid round-trips on every remote memory reference, we add a small fully-associative permission cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To track each outgoing request and its corresponding permission, we use a limited number of permission status holding registers.
Permissions are enforced (permission attributes and HWPIDs) at the response-end, where the response of a remote memory request is stalled via buffering until all its corresponding permission responses arrive.
This ensures all LD/ST are committed in-order.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we ensure the confidentiality of local pages.
A trusted process’ local memory addresses will have the A-bits tagged.
We add a memory encryption engine for encrypting these pages using the host’s private key (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{host}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{host}</annotation></semantics></math>).
The design therefore provides confidentiality for a trusted process’s local data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.4. </span>Extensions to the Fabric Manager (FM)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FM serves as the trusted coordination point for Space-Control.
Today, the only role of the FM is to set up the hardware tables for access control (§<a href="#S3" title="3. Background and Current Approaches ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
We extend its role to manage the permission table in the SDM, and, generate public labels for context authentication for each host.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Upon granting access to a process on a host, the FM issues a public label (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math>) that cryptographically binds a context (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>) to its permitted memory ranges.
The label is stored in the label section of the metadata and the entry, which has a defined range, is committed to the permission table.
In addition, the FM is also responsible for optimizing the permission table to minimize the total number of entries.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show the security analysis to validate our claims and justify our design choices.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Security Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our claim is this paper is that we guarantee the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span> of trusted context’s data and trust the disaggregation hardware for the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">availability</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Malicious host processes</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The host can spawn multiple processes, which tries to access the SDM.
Space-Control validates the trusted process’ context via the SPACE.
The context (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>) cannot be forged by any other process.
Any LD/ST generated by a trusted context is further checked by the permission checker.
The checker raises a fault when a non-trusted process (without the tagged <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span>) tries to access the remote memory region directly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Compromised system software</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume a strong threat model in which the OS may collude with untrusted processes to violate the confidentiality of a trusted process’s local and remote data.
We therefore use hardware-defined context (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>).
This prevents the OS from forging context labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although the OS can still modify page tables, such manipulation does not compromise remote memory.
If the kernel maps the SDM region into an untrusted process’s address space, SPACE rejects the access as authentication fails, and the permission checker blocks any unauthorized LD/ST.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A stronger threat arises when the kernel attempts to compromise the local memory <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span> of a trusted process.
Since local memory is untrusted in our model, the kernel may remap the page tables of a trusted process and a malicious process to the same physical memory region.
The trusted process remains unaware of the remapping and continues to use its local pages, while the malicious process gains read access to those same pages, violating <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent this attack, Space-Control employs a memory encryption engine that encrypts all local memory pages belonging to trusted contexts, identified by their tagged <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span>.
This ensures <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span> even if the OS aliases page tables.
However, while encrypted data prevents unauthorized reads, a malicious process could still overwrite ciphertext, affecting <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">integrity</span>.
When such writes are detected, Space-Control identifies the presence of compromised system software, satisfying our target threat model from §<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Threat Model ‣ 2. Problem Definition ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3. </span>Malicious DMA devices</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Space-Control assumes that SDM is accessed primarily via CPU LD/ST operations using <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">/dev/dax</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">MICRON, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Unlike system DRAM, a DAX region is not part of the system memory map.
It is exposed as a character device and mapped into a processes’ virtual address space.
Consequently, DMA-capable devices, such as GPUs and other accelerators, cannot directly access this region without explicit peer-to-peer mappings.
This distinction reinforces the gap we target, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, existing PASID-based Shared Virtual Addressing (SVA) mechanisms only apply to device-initiated DMA and do not cover CPU-originated accesses to SDM.
Therefore, hardware enforcement at the CPU egress point remains essential for guaranteeing process-level isolation in scenarios where the OS cannot be trusted.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Design Choices and Limitations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Space-Control introduces additional wiring overhead by tagging PA with the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span>.
The design is inspired by the C-Bit of AMDSEV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
We justify our design choice of extending the PA as today’s <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">state-of-the-art</span> architectures uses up to 57 out of 64 bits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghiti, 2021</a>)</cite>.
This allows flexibility of using the remaining unused bits to tag the address space.
Intel hardware already adds support to extend the upper 7 bits of the physical addresses in the kernel using Linear Address Masking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jonathan Corbet, 2022</a>)</cite>.
We allow up to 127 processes per host to ensure 64 bits address compatibility.
For simplicity, we choose to place the HWPID tag bits into the most significant bits of the address, which does not cause conflicts with the existing wiring for the TLB, the caches and ensures CXL compatibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An alternate method of protecting memory was via a reverse mapping table (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PA\rightarrow(VA,HWPID)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PA\rightarrow(VA,HWPID)</annotation></semantics></math>) per host, similar to AMDSEV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2016</a>)</cite>.
Such tables are also popular to identify hot cache lines for page migration in CXL-related prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>.
A reverse mapping from PA to (VA, PID) allows the hardware to infer which process owns a given physical page without modifying the address format.
However, given the current programming model for SDM, the base pointer to the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mmap</span> region is fixed and addresses are accessed based on offsets.
We chose to adopt a simple permission table where the mapping from PA to (host, (<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>)) is enough to ensure that the OS cannot modify the page table to remap the SDM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Based on our threat model, our design guarantees <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span> to the data at all times and <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">integrity</span> to the data in the SDM.
However, we rely on the CXL hardware to function correctly and provide <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">availability</span>.
This distinguishes Space-Control from a TEE.
If the trusted process is compromised at runtime, Space-Control fails both <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidentiality</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">integrity</span> guarantees.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe our evaluation platform and describe the workloads.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Disaggregated System Parameters</figcaption>
<div id="S6.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:138.0pt;height:95.9pt;vertical-align:-46.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-97.1pt,67.5pt) scale(0.415342710102423,0.415342710102423) ;">
<table id="S6.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C0C0C0;">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C0C0C0;">Parameter</span></th>
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C0C0C0;">Specification</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Number of hosts</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">up to 8 (incl. FM)</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Number of devices</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">1</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">CPU type</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">TimingSimpleCPU</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">CPU core count</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">8 (1 for the FM)</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Frequency</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">4 GHz</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">L1 cache (per core)</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">32 KiB + 32 KiB (I + D)</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">L2 cache (per core)</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">1 MiB</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">L3 cache (shared)</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">16 MiB</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Memory technology</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">DDR4 DRAM</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Memory frequency</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">2400 MHz</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.12" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Number of local memory channels</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">2</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.13" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Number of remote memory channels</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">4</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.14" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.14.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">Local memory size</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">16 GB</td></tr>
