<th id="S6.T2.2.1.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Operating System</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Ubuntu 22.04.4 (Jammy Jellyfish)</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T2.2.1.19" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.2.1.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">Kernel version</th>
<td id="S6.T2.2.1.19.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">6.9.9</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Setup and Workloads</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to understand the performance implications of Space-Control.
We used a modified version of GAPBS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beamer et al., 2017</a>)</cite> to share a graph across several hosts.
We have used gem5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lowe-Power et al., 2020</a>)</cite> to model the hosts for fidelity and SST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rodrigues et al., 2011</a>)</cite> to model the shared disaggregated memory and parallelize the simulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2012</a>)</cite>.
Space-Control hardware is implemented in gem5.
The modeling goal is to have a realistic CXL.mem implementation.
The CXL device, modeled in SST, is implemented as <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">timingDRAM</span> modeled as four-channel 2400 MHz DDR4 DRAM.
The latencies of translation and lookup of remote memory addresses is implemented at the gem5-SST bridge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2012</a>)</cite>, that connects the two simulators together.
The bridge is modified to support gem5-style checkpointing and KVM for fast-forwarding.
The timing parameters of the CXL protocol is based on prior characterization and research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aguilera et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are eight hosts. For simplicity, instead of modeling CXL.io for FM, we use shared CXL.mem for host and FM communication.
Hosts still interact with the permission table in userspace I/O <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koch, 2006</a>)</cite>.
Host 0 is responsible for allocating the data for the workloads.
For GAPBS, the rest of the hosts (1–6) run a specific kernel.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Experiments</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate Space-Control, we compare the performance slowdown due to permission checks at each LD/ST against a baseline CXL 3.0 system (<span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span>).
We show performance in terms of cycles per instruction (CPI) of the graph kernel’s <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">region of interest</span>.
The baseline is a system described in Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6. Evaluation ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> without any permission checks.
On a real CXL system, once a VA is translated to a PA, a CXL packet is created for the PA and sent to the CXL network until the memory device is reached.
In our <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span> system, we model the address translation correctly to establish a performance baseline for us to compare against Space-Control.
We simulate the first 4 billion cycles in the <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">region of interest</span> for each workload running on each host.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additional latencies are modeled for <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bit</span> tag comparison and memory encryption.
We use a hardware-efficient encryption engine implementation similar to prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2015</a>)</cite>, where encryption per cache-line is at most 1 cycle.
We repeat the experiments for sensitivity analysis with the permission cache.
In addition to evaluating Space-Control, we also implement several related works and compare the overall performance implications for enforcing permissions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss our results based on the evaluation strategy listed in §<a href="#S6" title="6. Evaluation ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
 This section is divided into three primary subsections that discuss the
<span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> performance,
<span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> overheads, and,
<span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(c)</span> compares Space-Control with existing works.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>Performance Analysis</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While context verification via SPACE incurs an insignificant one-time cost, we need to analyze the permission checker in details to understand the actual permission overhead.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.1. </span>Baseline and Scalability</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This experiment establishes the <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span> baseline, modeled after CXL 3.0, and quantifies Space-Control’s end-to-end overhead in the best-case sharing layout, when a single permission entry (<span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">space-control-1e</span>) spanning the SDM region.
This setting isolates the intrinsic cost of per-access authentication and permission checking from metadata fragmentation effects, and it lets us evaluate how the egress checker scales as more hosts participate.
For each local access we verify context, check for the <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A-bits</span> on the PA, and, encrypt the pages.
For each SDM access we consult the permission table for the current context (<span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HWPID</span>, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BASE_P</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We run GAPBS kernels while scaling the number of hosts accessing the same SDM region from 1 to 8 in powers of two.
We use four representative kernels: <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pr</span>, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bfs</span>, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bc</span>, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tc</span>; ordered from more regular to more random access patterns, and report CPI normalized to the <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span> baseline (no checks).
The single-entry configuration keeps the permission lookup path constant, so any change in CPI reflects the rate of permission lookups rather than longer lookup latency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F7.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S7.F7.2.1" class="ltx_text">


</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/scaling-1e.svg" id="S7.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/55;" width="110" height="55"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((a))</span> </span><span id="S7.F7.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Performance of the system with a single permission entry (best-case layout). CPI is normalized to the <span id="S7.F7.sf1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span> baseline</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/all-1e.svg" id="S7.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/55;" width="110" height="55"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((b))</span> </span><span id="S7.F7.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">CPI of all GAPBS kernels running concurrently on each host, normalized to the eight host <span id="S7.F7.sf2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span> baseline.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>Reported CPI in Space-Control normalized to <span id="S7.F7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S7.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ 7.1.1. Baseline and Scalability ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> shows the CPI for the four workloads when the number of systems are scaled.
Space-Control imposes a small overhead at one host (7.3% on average than <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span>) that grows sub-linearly to 12.1% at eight hosts.
This trend demonstrates that the permission checker scales without becoming a bottleneck, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> adding hosts increases the number of permission lookups, but the pipeline and caches keep lookup latency bounded.
The workload ordering matches intuition: <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pr</span> stays closest to baseline due to streaming locality, and, the least LLC misses; whereas the rest of the workloads incur more lookups and pay a larger penalty.
This reinforces the fact that locality and LLC misses drives overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the eight-host multiprogrammed setup of Figure <a href="#S7.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ 7.1.1. Baseline and Scalability ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, the same story persists, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pr</span> remains near baseline (0.6%), while <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cc</span> sits higher (23.4%) because <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cc</span> has a higher LLC miss rate which generates more irregular requests.
The key takeaway for deployment is that when tenants map few, contiguous SDM ranges, Space-Control’s cost is predictable even as tenancy scales.
The next subsection stress-tests the opposite extreme: <span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">worst-case</span> fragmentation to bound overhead when the permission table becomes deep and per-access lookups dominate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.2. </span>Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having established the single-entry baseline, we now stress the design with <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">worst-case</span> fragmentation to expose the cost of the sorted permission table when every access requires a deeper lookup.
This experiment isolates the central trade-off as we replace the storage overhead of flat tables with lower metadata but higher lookup time.
We then investigate the lookup overhead gets when the permission table is maximally deep.
Concretely, we compare the best case (a single permission entry spanning the SDM, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">space-control-1e</span>) to the worst case where every 4 KiB range has its own distinct entry (<span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wc</span>), so each SDM reference can trigger a binary search in a large table.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We repeat the baseline experiment under this worst-case layout and report normalized CPI alongside two indicators of lookup pressure: the binary-search occupancy (how many table probes per lookup) and the permission lookups per kilo-instructions (PLPKI).
This framing makes the mechanism’s behavior transparent as
<span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> CPI reflects end-to-end impact,
<span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> occupancy shows how deep the table is exercised, and,
<span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(c)</span> PLPKI captures how often the checker is invoked by the workload.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F8.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S7.F8.2.1" class="ltx_text">


</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/scaling-wc.svg" id="S7.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/55;" width="110" height="55"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((a))</span> </span><span id="S7.F8.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Reported CPI normalized to the baseline when the number of hosts sharing the SDM were increased in powers of two.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/plkpi.svg" id="S7.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/55;" width="110" height="55"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F8.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((b))</span> </span><span id="S7.F8.sf2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Reported PLPKI for a single permission entry case (<span id="S7.F8.sf2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1e</span>) and a complete table fragmentation case (<span id="S7.F8.sf2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wc</span>).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8. </span>Reported CPI in Space-Control normalized to <span id="S7.F8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span> when the memory is completely fragmented.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S7.F8.sf1" title="In Figure 8 ‣ 7.1.2. Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(a)</span></a> shows that fragmentation amplifies overhead in proportion to access irregularity: <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tc</span>, which intentionally skips relabeling and exhibits poor locality, rises the most (3.8<math id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vs. cxl</span>), whereas <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pr</span> remains close to baseline (5.7%).
Figure <a href="#S7.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 7.1.2. Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> corroborates the mechanism as <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tc</span> drives the highest binary-search occupancy, and Figure <a href="#S7.F8.sf2" title="In Figure 8 ‣ 7.1.2. Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a> confirms that PLPKI tracks this increase as more distinct, scattered references mean more frequent, deeper permission lookups.
By contrast, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bfs</span> and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bc</span> sit between <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text">pr</span> and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tc</span>, consistent with their frontier-driven irregularity but better reuse than <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tc</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/bin-occupancy.svg" id="S7.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/141;" width="476" height="141"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9. </span>The PDF of binary searches for a unique permission entry. The bars represent the average histogram sorted by the bins.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The takeaway is that Space-Control’s overhead under fragmentation is lookup-dominated and workload-dependent.
That is exactly the cost we chose in exchange for low storage overhead and OS-independent process isolation.
Importantly, the worst case is both diagnosable (high occupancy, high PLPKI) and actionable: operators can coalesce ranges or align shared allocations to cut table depth, and §<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS6" title="7.1.6. Performance Cache Efficacy ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.6</span></a> shows that even a small permission cache amortizes lookups and restores performance.
Together, these results bound the upper cost of fine-grained permissions and show how to steer deployments toward the single-entry or low-fragmentation regime, where Space-Control remains predictable and modest.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.3. </span>Memory-Access Split and Memory Bandwidth</h4>

<figure id="S7.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F10.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S7.F10.2.1" class="ltx_text">


</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F10.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/two-stacked-subplots.svg" id="S7.F10.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:129/43;" width="129" height="43"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F10.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((a))</span> </span><span id="S7.F10.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Stacked graphs showing the count of data traffic (darker shade) and permission traffic (lighter shade).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F10.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/bandwidth.svg" id="S7.F10.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:86/40;" width="86" height="40"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F10.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((b))</span> </span><span id="S7.F10.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Remote memory bandwidth usage reported by each host.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10. </span>Memory traffic analysis of Space-Control.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We quantify how much fabric activity comes from permission packets versus data packets, because both contend for the same CXL links and device queues.
Figure <a href="#S7.F10.sf1" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 7.1.3. Memory-Access Split and Memory Bandwidth ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(a)</span></a> shows that, under fragmented tables, irregular kernels (<span id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tc</span>, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bc</span>) drive a large share of permission packets.
Figure <a href="#S7.F10.sf2" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 7.1.3. Memory-Access Split and Memory Bandwidth ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(b)</span></a> reports per-host bandwidth of the remote memory.
As the permission share falls (<span id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1e vs. wc</span>), hosts experience fewer permission-induced stalls and sustain higher effective data throughput, while the device spends proportionally more cycles servicing data than probing the table.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Space-Control’s cost is governed by lookup frequency, not bulky control transfers.
Two practical levers, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> keep shared ranges coarse and provision a small permission cache, can restore a fabric profile where permission traffic is a minor control-plane slice and data bandwidth tracks application demand.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.4. </span>Breakdown of Performance Contributors</h4>

<figure id="S7.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F11.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S7.F11.2.1" class="ltx_text">


</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F11.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/all-stall.svg" id="S7.F11.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/55;" width="110" height="55"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F11.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((a))</span> </span><span id="S7.F11.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Stall latency across all the GAPBS workloads.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F11.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/breakdown-stacked.svg" id="S7.F11.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/55;" width="110" height="55"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F11.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((b))</span> </span><span id="S7.F11.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Stacked latencies of the three factors contributing towards performance slowdown.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11. </span>Performance breakdown analysis.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the source of the overhead, we instrument the permission checker pipeline and attribute runtime to three components: <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> permission request creation, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> permission lookup latency, and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(c)</span> enforcement latency, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> the time a LD/ST response is stalled until all corresponding permission entries arrive.
Creation is largely depended on the circuit design and is small; the other two depend on the table organization and on how many lookups each workload triggers, tying this analysis directly to §<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS2" title="7.1.2. Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.2</span></a>’s fragmentation results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S7.F11.sf2" title="In Figure 11 ‣ 7.1.4. Breakdown of Performance Contributors ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(b)</span></a> shows that enforcement stalls dominate the slowdown (99.95% in our configuration), while A-bit comparisons are negligible (0.003%).
Intuitively, irregular workloads and fragmented tables inflate both lookup depth and lookup rate, which lengthens stalls because more responses must be matched with more (and deeper) permission entries before commit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.5. </span>Performance Enforcement Latency</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">§<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS4" title="7.1.4. Breakdown of Performance Contributors ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.4</span></a> shows enforcing permissions impacts the response path the most.
We issue LD/ST and permission requests out-of-order, but a LD/ST response cannot commit until all of its corresponding permission entries have arrived.
We call the resulting wait time as the enforcement or the stalling latency.
Figure <a href="#S7.F11.sf1" title="In Figure 11 ‣ 7.1.4. Breakdown of Performance Contributors ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11(a)</span></a> reports the average tail latency across workloads.
It reflects the time the pipeline spends buffering responses to preserve in-order architectural commit while Space-Control verifies access rights.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/tail-latency-histogram.svg" id="S7.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/139;" width="476" height="139"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12. </span>Histogram of permission enforcement latency.
The line represents the PDF of the latency distribution.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consistent with §<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS2" title="7.1.2. Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.2</span></a>, more random access patterns yield longer stalls because they trigger more and deeper permission lookups.
The histograms in Figure <a href="#S7.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 7.1.5. Performance Enforcement Latency ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> make this visible, where kernels like <span id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tc</span> and <span id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bc</span> exhibit heavier stalls than <span id="S7.SS1.SSS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pr</span>.
The takeaway is that enforcement latency is a lookup-dominated cost; reducing lookup frequency (fewer, coarser ranges) or amortizing lookups (a small permission cache) directly shortens the stall time.
This sets up §<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS6" title="7.1.6. Performance Cache Efficacy ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.6</span></a>, where we show that modest caching materially reduces enforcement time and restores end-to-end responsiveness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.6. </span>Performance Cache Efficacy</h4>

<figure id="S7.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F13.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S7.F13.2.1" class="ltx_text">


</span></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F13.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/miss-ratio.svg" id="S7.F13.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:86/58;" width="86" height="58"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F13.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((a))</span> </span><span id="S7.F13.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Reported permission cache miss ratio with increasing permission cache size in powers of two.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S7.F13.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/cache-cpi.svg" id="S7.F13.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:134/54;" width="134" height="54"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S7.F13.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">((b))</span> </span><span id="S7.F13.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Reported CPI of the system with increasing number of cache sizes. Numbers are normalized to a Space-Control system with complete memory fragmentation without caching.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13. </span>Measured performance efficacy of Space-Control with a permission cache. All systems are assumed to be completely fragmented, as mentioned in §<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS2" title="7.1.2. Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.2</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous sections showed that Space-Control’s overhead is lookup-driven (§<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS2" title="7.1.2. Sensitivity to Permission Table Fragmentation ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.2</span></a>) and expresses as enforcement stalls (§<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS5" title="7.1.5. Performance Enforcement Latency ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.5</span></a> and §<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS4" title="7.1.4. Breakdown of Performance Contributors ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.4</span></a>).
A small permission cache amortizes repeated lookups as it reduces PLPKI and binary-search depth, which shortens enforcement stall latency and restores throughput (§<a href="#S7.SS1.SSS3" title="7.1.3. Memory-Access Split and Memory Bandwidth ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We sweep cache sizes from 0.5 KiB (8 entries) to 64 KiB (1024 entries) and report miss ratio (Figure <a href="#S7.F13.sf1" title="In Figure 13 ‣ 7.1.6. Performance Cache Efficacy ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13(a)</span></a>) and CPI normalized to the uncached worst-case fragmentation (Figure <a href="#S7.F13.sf2" title="In Figure 13 ‣ 7.1.6. Performance Cache Efficacy ‣ 7.1. Performance Analysis ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13(b)</span></a>).
In this stress setting, where there are distinct entry per 4 KiB range, the cache targets the mechanism’s worst behavior (many deep lookups).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There is a pronounced elbow in the cache-benefit curve: a modest permission cache delivers most of the gain.
With just 2 KiB of cache, the hit rate reaches 99.9% and the CPI overhead improves by 2.3<math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> over the uncached Space-Control configuration on GAPBS.
The sharp drop in miss ratio from 1 KiB to 2 KiB stems from the way the sorted permission table is searched: a binary search repeatedly revisits a small set of internal decision nodes across lookups, while the leaf (the final permission entry for the specific range) is the part most likely to be uncached.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a 16 GiB shared memory, the binary search touches at most 24 nodes at worst.
A 2 KiB cache holds 32 entries, which exceeds the depth and keeps most internal nodes resident.
Only the leaf entries tends to miss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This scaling intuition generalizes.
For 1 TiB shared memory (up to <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lg(2^{40})=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>40</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lg(2^{40})=40</annotation></semantics></math> probes), 4 KiB cache (64 entries) exceeds the worst-case search depth, amortizing the lookup cost.
This makes cache auto-tuning straightforward, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> choose a capacity that meets or slightly exceeds <math id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lg(\text{table size})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>table size</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lg(\text{table size})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the CXL baseline, a 16 KiB cache leaves only 3.3% marginal performance overhead.
Consistent with our previous results, caching also trims enforcement stalls, and benefits show diminishing returns beyond 4 KiB in the worst-case fragmentation for most workloads.
A small permission cache converts Space-Control from lookup-bound and stall-heavy to data-dominant, aligning with the design flow we established so far.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.1.7. </span>Permission Revocation</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Updating permissions follows the CXL’s back invalidate snoop (<span id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BISnp</span>) protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>)</cite>.
When a host updates the permission table via a new entry and the entry is approved and pushed into the table by the FM, a CXL <span id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BISnp</span> is sent on the network.
Permission caches on hosts caching that entry will automatically invalidate the permission entry from its own cache.
The latency of performing a permission revocation is the same as CXL’s <span id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BISnp</span> latency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One limitation of permission revocation is that it fails to prevent on-flight instructions getting committed.
While Space-Control ensures response-side permission checks, meaning that access control is done after receiving responses instead of apriori approvals, there still exists a tiny window where an attacker can read (not write) data from the SDM.
This problem however is the CXL’s cache coherence problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, which is orthogonal to our work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>Hardware Overhead</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Space-Control, a memory range of an entry can be of any arbitrary length.
For practicality and future-proofing, we limit the minimum length to be 4 KiB, the same as the smallest unit of an OS-level page size.
This implies that in the worst case where every 4 KiB page of the SDM will have a 64 Byte entry, resulting in a maximum of 1.56% overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use 32 permission miss status holding registers to keep track of all the permission entry misses.
The response buffer for permission enforcement holds up to 32 responses.
This totals 4 KiB of additional SRAM storage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The cryptographic engine follows a similar implementation to prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Juliato and Gebotys, 2011</a>)</cite>.
We add two 64-bit shadow registers <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">label</span> and <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">authentication result</span> at the CPU.
SPACE uses minimal logic and wiring overhead for comparing contexts and generating labels.
The total SRAM storage needed to maintain the key (64 bits), counter (64 bits), free HWPID list (128 entries) and public label, <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span> <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{exp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{exp}</annotation></semantics></math> (64 bits) is 1.048 KiB.
The permission checker uses five comparators to generate permission requests and enforce permissions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>Comparison to Prior Mechanisms</h3>

<figure id="S7.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06951v1/comparison.svg" id="S7.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14. </span>CPI of Space-Control and several other related works. The CPI is normalized to the baseline <span id="S7.F14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span>. None of the systems have any performance enhancing permission caches for a fair comparison.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span> baseline, we also compare the performance slowdown to an ideal <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flat-table</span> structure similar to Border-Control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olson et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, and, other related works: <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deact-like</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mondrian-ext</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Witchel et al., 2002</a>)</cite>.
Figure <a href="#S7.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 7.3. Comparison to Prior Mechanisms ‣ 7. Results ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> shows the CPI normalized to the baseline across all the other techniques.
The <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flat-table</span> structure requires at most one permission table entry lookup per remote memory access.
Compared to the baseline, the slowdown is 13.1%, which is comparable to <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">space-control-1e</span>.
<span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">space-control-1e</span> slightly performs better as there is a single entry at a fixed location unlike <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flat-tables</span>, where each PPN reads its corresponding permissions from a different region of the SDM.
The total storage on the other hand, is given by:</p>
<table id="S7.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}N_{hosts}\times M_{processes}\times P_{pages}\times ENTRY_{size}\\
=256\times 128\times(16\text{GiB}/4\text{KiB})\times(2b/8)=32\text{GiB}\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>128</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GiB</mtext></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>KiB</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GiB</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}N_{hosts}\times M_{processes}\times P_{pages}\times ENTRY_{size}\\
=256\times 128\times(16\text{GiB}/4\text{KiB})\times(2b/8)=32\text{GiB}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The storage overhead is 200% than the total remote memory size and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="128.2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>128.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128.2\times</annotation></semantics></math> larger than Space-Control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deact-like</span> is our implementation of DeACT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
We maintain an address-permission mapping per host per 4 KiB SDM page instead of 1 GiB page for a fair comparison.
We adopt the table for sharing 16 GiB of memory across 256 hosts, where each host can have up to 128 processes.
The total storage for sharing one process is given as:</p>
<table id="S7.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SIZE_{mapping\_table}+SIZE_{sharing\_bitmap}=0.156\text{GiB}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.156</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GiB</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SIZE_{mapping\_table}+SIZE_{sharing\_bitmap}=0.156\text{GiB}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which is only 0.9% total overhead.
However, DeACT’s threat model does not trust the OS but lets the OS manage the page tables for all the processes sharing SDM. If we scale to 128 processes outside the host OS’ control, the replication of the mapping table and the bitmap takes 20 GiB of metadata, which is 125% the size of the total remote memory size, and <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="80.1\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80.1\times</annotation></semantics></math> larger than Space-Control.
The average CPI of <span id="S7.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deact-like</span> is 32.66% higher than <span id="S7.SS3.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">space-control-1e</span> as there are at most two permission entry lookups in DeACT: the owner mapping entry and the shared bitmap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also extend Mondrian to support memory disaggregation (<span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mondrian-ext</span>).
The original Mondrian solution does not distinguish between remote or local addresses as mondrian permission tables are setup for every process.
We maintain at least two domains: <span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> local memory processes and <span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> the real workload, which spawns across both the local and the remote memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Domains in Mondrian and permission entries in Space-Control are user-defined.
For the workloads we have, one domain or entry in Space-Control is enough to show the principle of least privilege is satisfied in these works.
However, for complete fairness, we compare a single entry as the best case (<span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1e</span>), and, every 4 KiB entry as the worst case scenario (<span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wc</span>) against <span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cxl</span>, <span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flat-table</span> and <span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deact</span>.
Mondrian maintains two protection domains: <span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> for the SDM region, and, <span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> single permission entry per host for the local memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of storage overhead, both Mondrian and Space-Control are similar.
Mondrian’s sorted segment table entries are smaller however, they need to replicate their permission table (sorted segment table) per host, linearly increasing the total storage overhead.
Space-Control performs 4.3<math id="S7.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> better than <span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mondrian-ext</span> in terms of performance as the scope of Space-Control is only for shared memory accesses.
In other words, Space-Control extends the principles of Mondrian for an untrusted OS in the era of CXL and shared disaggregated memory.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory disaggregation.</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">§<a href="#S3" title="3. Background and Current Approaches ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> gives a brief introduction to disaggregated memory.
Prior works like DeACT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommareddy et al., 2021</a>)</cite> relies on Gen-Z’s host-side zMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Interconnect, 2017</a>)</cite> and two-stage translation to decouple address translation from permission checks.
CXL removes the host zMMU and reuses the CPU’s TLB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>)</cite>, so Space-Control enforces authorization after the last-level cache.
RDMA-based works like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> is orthogonal to CXL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OS-managed mechanisms.</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OS-managed protections via <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">namespaces</span> and <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cgroups</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>)</cite>, MPK and ARM MTE provide intra-host protection but assume a trusted kernel.
LegoOS scales disaggregation but also trusts the OS for isolation.
Space-Control removes the OS from the enforcement path (§3). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">ARM, Technical report</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shan et al., 2018</a>)</cite>
Border Control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Olson et al., 2015</a>)</cite> sandboxes accelerators with page-level checks under a trusted OS.
Space-Control is inspired by this work and builds on this insight by treating untrusted hosts analogous to untrusted accelerators, enforcing access control through a hardware permission checker integrated into the memory access path.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Domain/capability designs.</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mondrian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Witchel et al., 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Witchel et al., 2005</a>)</cite> introduces the concept of domains and uses a sorted protection table to trade-off storage for higher lookup latency. Space-Control adopts the sorted-table idea but enforces at the CPU egress with process-level isolation and an untrusted OS (§<a href="#S4" title="4. Space-Control ‣ Space-Control: Process-Level Isolation for Sharing CXL-based Disaggregated Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
CHERI-style capability systems offer principled fine-grained authority but require pervasive ISA/compiler/OS changes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Woodruff et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stark et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watson et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.
Bresniker <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bresniker et al., 2019</a>)</cite> proposed the addition of memory-side capability check to extend CHERI to the disaggregated memory space.
The memory-side capability check is for address range check per process per host, creating a high metadata overhead and restricting scalability.
Furthermore, it requires a trusted OS for process coordination. We target deployability on existing CPUs and CXL fabrics using simple techniques like <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LD_PRELOAD</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TEEs and enclave models.</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TEEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> provide isolation and attestation but are ill-suited for multi-host shared ranges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>)</cite> and add non-trivial overhead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akram et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
Sharing memory enclaves on a single host is an active research topic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, however commercially available TEEs are yet to adopt it.
Minerva <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alwadi et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is an extension to the TEE design for disaggregated memory, where the authors made a fault-tolerant metadata management scheme for disaggregated memory.
ShieldCXL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposes an oblivious TEE for CXL systems.
Li <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposes an efficient technique to encrypt CXL flits for designing a TEE on a CXL system.
Space-Control enforces authorization without enclaves.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work addresses a critical gap in disaggregated memory systems: the lack of process-level isolation in shared disaggregated memory.
Space-Control provides a scalable hardware-software co-design that enforces fine-grained access control without relying on the OS.
The design remains fully compatible with existing virtual memory abstractions.
Our design uses a space-efficient metadata structure and leverages the CXL fabric manager to scale across many hosts and processes, incurring only 1.56% memory overhead and 3.3% performance overhead.
By reconciling strong security with system scalability, Space-Control establishes a practical foundation for secure disaggregated memory.
For future work, we plan on extending Space-Control into the TEE realm with shared enclaves across hosts, and providing <span id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">availability</span> at all times.</p>
</div>
</section>
